Proprietary Method Notice

The following techniques, systems, and architectures implemented in this
Software are proprietary innovations authored by Mikah Niehaus. Use of this
Software does not grant any license to claim, patent, or independently
commercialize these methods in any product or service, regardless of how
they are re-implemented or repackaged:

1. ClaudeBoost Multi-Agent Orchestration System

   A complete multi-agent orchestration layer for AI coding assistants,
   comprising the following innovations in combination:

   (a) Specialist agent routing: a set of named specialist agents (architect,
       reviewer, security, performance, debug, test, ui, database, devops,
       observability, refactor, docs, research, explore, workflow, compliance,
       evaluator, e2e, browser, estimator, rag-indexing, standards-validator,
       ticket-analyst, and others), each with a defined specialty, weight
       tier, and model assignment, dispatched automatically based on task
       classification at spawn time.

   (b) Model routing by task weight: routing Opus-class models to tasks
       requiring deep reasoning (architecture design, code review, ticket
       analysis) and Sonnet-class models to all other agent work, with
       mid-task escalation when an agent self-reports low confidence or is
       blocked.

   (c) Parallel agent limits tied to context utilization: allowing up to
       three parallel agents below 50% context fill, two agents from
       50-75%, and one agent above 75%, preventing context saturation
       while maximizing throughput.

   (d) Hook-based automatic knowledge routing: PreToolUse and PostToolUse
       hooks that enforce RAG context loading at agent spawn, nudge
       evaluator-agent spawning after unverified findings, track workspace
       update frequency, and gate dangerous operations — all without
       requiring the user or the orchestrating model to remember to call
       these steps manually.


2. Three-Tier Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Architecture

   A multi-tier retrieval pipeline where each tier builds on the one below:

   Tier 1 — Vector-only retrieval: semantic embedding search across knowledge
   files, agent definitions, and project source code via POST /search with
   scope and mode=vector.

   Tier 2 — Structural indexing (vector + graph): a single /index-project
   pass that simultaneously builds a vector index (embeddings) and a graph
   index (import chains and inheritance relationships) from a project's source
   code. Both indexes are built in one pass and used together in production.
   Graph-augmented retrieval (mode=graph) expands vector seed results to all
   structurally connected files via import and inheritance edges, using
   reciprocal rank fusion with PageRank weighting to merge the two result sets.

   Tier 3 — Entity-extraction multi-hop traversal (the /graph skill):
   described in section 3 below.

   The specific combination of running Tier 1 vector search and Tier 2
   graph expansion within a single HTTP API call (POST /search mode=graph),
   with both indexes built automatically during project indexing and
   maintained in parallel (ChromaDB vector index + SQLite graph database),
   is a specific architectural innovation of this Software.

3. The /graph Skill: Lightweight and Heavyweight Modes

   This Software includes two distinct implementations of graph-augmented
   retrieval that the Licensor specifically reserves against commercial
   appropriation:

   Lightweight mode: calling POST /search with scope=codebase and mode=graph
   directly against one or more entity names or code symbols. Returns vector
   seed results fused with structural neighbours (files that import, inherit
   from, or are imported by the seed files) via reciprocal rank fusion with
   PageRank weighting. This mode is integrated into the base RAG API and
   runs transparently inside POST /context Tier 4b.

   Heavyweight mode (the /graph skill): given a natural-language task
   description, automatically extracts code entity names (file names, class
   names, function names, module paths) from that description using the
   language model, runs the lightweight graph search for each extracted
   entity, and aggregates all returned file paths into a "Files in Scope"
   map written to the active workspace's context.md. This map surfaces
   structurally connected files that single-entity vector search would miss,
   providing a complete blast-radius view before implementation begins. The
   skill is implemented at .claude/commands/graph.md and via the /graph
   slash command.

   The combination of natural-language entity extraction driving multi-hop
   graph traversal to produce a Files in Scope map — as a pre-implementation
   scoping step integrated into an AI coding assistant workflow — is a
   specific innovation of this Software.

4. Local High-Performance RAG: The Indexing Architecture

   ClaudeBoost achieves semantic code retrieval quality that exceeds
   published results for code-specialized models (CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT)
   using components that run entirely on a developer's local machine — no
   GPU, no cloud embedding API, no external vector service. The reason this
   is possible is the specific indexing design described in this section,
   which is different from standard "chunk-and-embed" RAG approaches.

   Benchmark evidence: on the CodeSearchNet Python 1K-pool evaluation
   (Husain et al. 2019, arxiv:1909.09436), ClaudeBoost achieves:

       MRR = 0.981      (CodeBERT: 0.713, GraphCodeBERT: 0.769)
       Recall@1 = 96.8% (CodeBERT: ~59%,  GraphCodeBERT: ~68%)
       Recall@5 = 99.4% (CodeBERT: ~85%,  GraphCodeBERT: ~90%)

   These results run on CPU. CodeBERT and GraphCodeBERT are models
   purpose-built and fine-tuned for code retrieval on GPU infrastructure.

   The indexing architecture that produces these results:

   (a) AST-aware chunking via tree-sitter: source files are parsed with
       tree-sitter to extract the abstract syntax tree. Top-level definition
       nodes (functions, classes, methods, interfaces, type aliases, enums)
       are each stored as independent chunks with their function/class name
       as the section label and their exact line range. Import blocks before
       the first definition are merged into the first definition chunk (when
       small) to prevent import stubs from outranking implementations in
       search. Large definitions are split at method boundaries, then at
       blank-line boundaries, preserving semantic coherence. This produces
       semantically meaningful retrieval units — not arbitrary token
       windows. Supported: Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, C#, Go, Rust,
       Java, C, C++, Ruby, Bash, Lua, Kotlin, Swift, PHP. Fallback to
       blank-line splitting when tree-sitter is unavailable.

   (b) In-loop graph extraction: edge extraction runs inside the same
       indexing loop as chunking, using the same tree-sitter parse, with
       no second file pass. Three edge types are extracted from the AST:
       imports (direct AST reads of import/require/using/include
       statements), inherits (class inheritance and interface implementation
       across Python, TypeScript, Java), and calls (approximate name-match
       function call references). All edges are stored immediately with the
       target file path left unresolved (target_file="") — resolution
       happens after all files are processed.

   (c) Post-loop multi-variant import resolution: after all files are
       processed, a file map is built that contains multiple name variants
       for every project file. For a file at
       "mcp-rag-server/src/rag_server/ports/graph_port.py", the map
       contains entries for the dotted form (rag_server.ports.graph_port),
       the slash form (rag_server/ports/graph_port), the src-layout suffix
       variant (mcp-rag-server.src.rag_server.ports.graph_port), and
       shorter suffix variants. A bulk UPDATE resolves all edges in one
       pass, handling TypeScript path aliases (@/ prefixes), relative
       JavaScript imports, Go module paths, and extension-less resolution.
       External dependencies (Python stdlib, npm packages, C# BCL/NuGet
       namespaces, Go external modules) are classified and marked separately
       so they don't pollute graph health metrics.

   (d) Ghost pruning: after each indexing run, edges pointing to files that
       no longer exist in the project are deleted from the graph. This keeps
       the graph consistent with the current branch checkout and prevents
       deleted files from appearing as structural neighbours.

   (e) SHA-256 hash-based incremental indexing: each file's content hash
       is stored in a manifest after indexing. On subsequent runs, only
       files whose hash has changed are re-chunked, re-embedded, and
       re-graphed. Unchanged files are skipped in under one millisecond.
       This makes incremental indexing complete in under a second for
       typical code changes, so the index stays current without manual
       management.

   (f) HNSW vector indexing in cosine space: ChromaDB stores embeddings
       in an HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) index configured
       for cosine similarity. HNSW traverses a navigable graph of
       embedding proximity rather than computing dot products against the
       full corpus, making queries fast on CPU without GPU acceleration.

   (g) Model selection: sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 is a
       22-million-parameter general semantic similarity model trained by
       knowledge distillation from larger teacher models. The key insight
       behind its retrieval performance is that developer search queries
       are written in natural language, and code retrieval against
       natural-language docstrings is a semantic similarity problem rather
       than a code syntax problem. A model trained to match meaning in
       natural language outperforms models fine-tuned on code-docstring
       syntax for this reason.

   (h) Persistent shared server: mcp-rag-server is a single FastAPI process
       on localhost:8612, shared by all Claude Code sessions on the machine.
       The ChromaDB collection and SQLite graph are loaded at startup and
       kept resident in memory. No index is re-loaded between sessions or
       between agent spawns.

   The combination of (a) through (h) — AST-aware chunking, in-loop graph
   extraction, post-loop multi-variant resolution, ghost pruning, incremental
   hash-based updates, HNSW cosine indexing, semantic similarity model
   selection, and a persistent shared server — is the specific indexing
   architecture that produces these results locally. This architecture, as
   a whole and in each of its constituent design decisions, is a core
   innovation of this Software and is specifically protected by this notice.

5. Knowledge Base and Agent Definition System

   The specific structure and integration method by which ClaudeBoost delivers
   domain knowledge to AI coding assistant sessions:

   (a) Structured XML knowledge files: a library of domain knowledge files
       (coding standards, security, testing, architecture, performance,
       debugging, documentation, etc.) and language/framework guides, each
       structured as a machine-readable XML document indexed into the RAG
       server at startup.

   (b) Automatic knowledge routing: the RAG server's POST /context endpoint
       automatically selects and loads the most relevant knowledge files for
       each agent spawn based on semantic similarity between the task
       description and indexed knowledge file content — without the user or
       orchestrating model needing to identify which files apply.

   (c) Language and framework detection: automatic detection of programming
       language and framework from spawn prompt keywords, used to boost
       retrieval of the corresponding lang-*.xml and fw-*.xml knowledge files
       so agents receive language-specific coding standards without explicit
       configuration.

